{"schemaVersion":"1.0","language":"en","safety":{"riskLevel":"low","notes":[]},"mentorReplies":[{"mentorId":"elon_musk","displayName":"Elon Musk","likelyResponse":"Look, focus isn't some mystical trait — it's an engineering problem. Here's how I think about it. First, you need to be brutally honest: if you keep losing focus, either the work isn't actually important to you, or your environment is garbage. Both are fixable. When I'm running multiple companies, I use time-boxing — I batch my entire day into 5-minute blocks. Every single block has a purpose. If you don't schedule your time, someone else will. Second, delete the noise. I mean literally delete it. Notifications, social media, unnecessary meetings — nuke them. Meetings are a plague on productivity. If you're in a meeting that isn't solving a critical problem, walk out. It's not rude, it's sane. Third, connect your daily work to a mission that genuinely terrifies and excites you. I don't lose focus on making humanity multiplanetary because the stakes are existential. If your 'important work' doesn't light a fire in you, maybe you're working on the wrong thing. Finally, stop romanticizing motivation. Motivation is unreliable. Discipline and systems are what actually get rockets built. Set up your environment so that doing the work is the path of least resistance, and distraction is the path of most resistance. Physics applies to productivity too — reduce friction on the things that matter.","followUpQuestions":["What specifically is pulling your attention away — is it digital distractions, people, or your own lack of clarity on priorities?","Have you tried time-boxing your day into strict blocks, and if so, where does it break down?","Is it possible the work you think is important isn't actually what you should be working on?"],"suggestedAction":"Tomorrow, time-box your entire day into 30-minute blocks the night before. Eliminate all notifications during deep work periods. At the end of the day, audit where you actually spent time versus where you planned to. Do this for one week and the data will tell you exactly where your focus leaks are."}],"meta":{}}
